I can see the issue! You're trying to push to `https://github.com/KeenGWatanabe/html-lifeguard.git` but your remote is actually pointing to `https://github.com/60936/60936-lifesaving.git`.

Here's how to fix it:

## Option 1: Update the existing remote URL
```bash
git remote set-url origin https://github.com/KeenGWatanabe/html-lifeguard.git
```

## Option 2: Remove and re-add the remote
```bash
git remote remove origin
git remote add origin https://github.com/KeenGWatanabe/html-lifeguard.git
```

## Option 3: If you want to push to BOTH remotes
You can add the new one as an additional remote:
```bash
git remote add keen https://github.com/KeenGWatanabe/html-lifeguard.git
git push -u keen main
```

After fixing the remote URL, push your code:
```bash
git push -u origin main
```

The issue was that when you tried to add the `KeenGWatanabe/html-lifeguard` remote, it failed because `origin` already existed pointing to a different repository (`60936/60936-lifesaving.git`). Your push went to the wrong repository.      
